  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Source of record</h6>
        <p>Bank for International Settlements statistics: total credit (WS_TC), credit-to-GDP gaps (WS_CREDIT_GAP), debt service ratios (WS_DSR), global liquidity indicators (WS_GLI), US dollar exchange rates (WS_XRU) and central bank policy rates (WS_CBPOL), read from India Atlas's archived copy of the BIS statistics. Data vintage <b id="prov-vintage">–</b>.</p>
        <p>Checked by hand: India's private credit, 105.1% of GDP in January to March 2026, households 49.1 and companies 56.0, government 83.5, gap 4.3 points, as published on the BIS data portal.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Method</h6>
        <p>Values are as BIS publishes them. Rupee amounts are converted from ₹ billion to ₹ lakh crore; the rupee value of dollar debt is computed from the quarter's average exchange rate. The comparison ranks the sum of the three borrowers' debts, with government at nominal value. Nothing is estimated.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions and breaks</h6>
        <p>BIS revises these series as national authorities revise theirs, and each quarterly update can change several years of history. Break adjustment is BIS's own; BIS flags a break in households' unadjusted credit in January to March 2024. India's private credit after the last financial-accounts quarter is a BIS estimate, marked as such in every download.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Version 1.0, built October 2026 from BIS data to January to March 2026. Revised each quarter when BIS publishes. BIS does not endorse this guide.</p>
      </div>
